  nonce: b4c1  # b4c1
  data:  # b4c1
    kind: work_item  # b4c1
    title: "c: sys/socket send*/recv* are net_* only; an AF_UNIX / socketpair fd is process-local IPC (0012 tmux) -- dual rows + socket-family stamp from the fd's binding"  # b4c1
    status: todo_hard  # b4c1
    priority: 2  # b4c1
    parent:  # b4c1
    tags:  # b4c1
    - catalogue  # b4c1
    - io_boundary  # b4c1
    - taint  # b4c1
    - c  # b4c1
    isbefore: []  # b4c1
    duplicate_of: []  # b4c1
    not_duplicate_of: []  # b4c1
    pr_ref:  # b4c1
    description: "FOUND 2026-09-03 from INV-nular's 09-03 entry (measurement 0012). c.yaml:90 declares sys/socket.{connect,send,sendto,sendmsg} under net_send with NO boundary_ruling, while the very next row (unistd.write) carries call_site_undecidable for exactly this reason. tmux:host-description-no-network:6 sends over an fd that is always AF_UNIX (client.c:122, server.c:123, proc.c:364 socketpair) -- process-local IPC, the catalogue's own ipc_send zone -- and was reported as a network send (0012 VACUOUS:KIND-MISDECLARED). The same shape holds on the read side: sys/socket.{recv,recvfrom,recvmsg} are net_recv only, and an AF_UNIX recv is ipc_recv.\n\nWHAT DECIDES IT is the socket FAMILY, established where the fd was created: socket(AF_UNIX, ...) / socketpair(...) -> process-local; socket(AF_INET|AF_INET6, ...) -> network. That is the same \"the fd's nature is established where it was opened\" rule INV-vaduk wrote on unistd.read/write, one hop back through the fd variable -- the hop c.py already performs for fgets's stream argument (test_c_stream_argument_origin.py).\n\nDESIGN. (1) Rows: send/sendto/sendmsg dual net_send + ipc_send, recv/recvfrom/recvmsg dual net_recv + ipc_recv, boundary_ruling call_site_undecidable, abstains_to = today's answer (net_*) so unstamped calls classify as before. connect stays net_send-only (it transmits nothing but a connection; ADR-0049's deferred shape -- separate question). (2) c.py stamps io_target_kind on the send/recv from the fd argument's binding: socketpair(...) or socket(AF_UNIX/AF_LOCAL, ...) -> `pipe` (process-local); socket(AF_INET/AF_INET6, ...) -> `net_stream`; anything else abstains. Both kinds are introduced by the sibling go item; land that first or together. (3) A/B on the c/cpp repos in the corpus (tmux, tmate, shellcheck's C bits), every moved row read back.\n\nDEPENDS ON the sibling write-side item for the vocabulary (`pipe`, `net_stream`) and the write-side seam."  # b4c1
    fields: {}  # b4c1
- op: discuss  # e11b
  at: '2026-09-06T11:27:04Z'  # e11b
  by: agent  # e11b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # e11b
  clock: 2  # e11b
  nonce: e11b  # e11b
  message: "PREMISE MEASURED BEFORE BUILDING, 2026-09-06 (arm A on dev ef54494949, twelve C repositories that call send/sendto/sendmsg/recv/recvfrom/recvmsg, 324 sites; instrument ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/baran_sockets_09062026/).\n\n1. THE ITEM'S OWN INSTANCE DOES NOT MOVE UNDER ITS OWN DESIGN. tmux's two live sites (compat/imsg-buffer.c sendmsg/recvmsg) take the descriptor as a function PARAMETER; the socket() calls the item cites (client.c:122, server.c:123, proc.c:364) are two functions away. The same-function last-binding hop -- the instrument the item names, INV-vaduk's \"established where it was opened\" applied one hop back -- abstains there by design. Moving tmux's row needs the cross-function hop (WI-famig's summaries) or a repository-level \"no AF_INET socket anywhere\" inference, which is the human adjudicator's move in 0012 and a different, false-all-clear-direction mechanism. Not built; recorded.\n\n2. THE POPULATION THAT CAN MOVE. Descriptor argument shapes over 324 sites: bare identifier 222, struct field 77, socketpair element 10, inline call 7, other 8. Of the identifiers, a same-FILE socket()/socketpair()/accept() binding exists for: passt (unix 11, inet 4, other-family 6), fluent-bit (unix 22, inet 17, accept 6, other 10), haproxy (unix 4, other 11), firejail (inet 8, other 4), dbus-broker (unix 3), gvisor, libvfio-user. So the stamp has a corpus population even though tmux is not in it.\n\n3. TWO REACH GAPS FOUND BY THE CENSUS, filed rather than chased:\n   (a) bare `send` and `recv` are in c.yaml ambiguous_names (\"socket send vs project-local send()\"), and the C analyzer fills no module slot from #include, so every bare send/recv site is `c:external:0-0:send:unresolved` and gate_named_entry refuses it: dbus-broker 15/17, haproxy 28/48, passt 23/43 sites classify as NOTHING today. Only sendto/sendmsg/recvfrom/recvmsg reach the catalogue. That is INV-linub's class in C (a catalogued call site that cannot reach its row without receiver/module evidence); the resolvable form is \"the file includes <sys/socket.h> and defines no send\" -- recall work, Phase 6.\n   (b) fluent-bit: 116 of 162 send/recv sites RESOLVE by name to a vendored platform shim (lib/wasm-micro-runtime .../platform/nuttx/nuttx_platform.c:258 sendto), so the call graph binds every sendto() in the repository to one vendored wrapper -- INV-mozas's wrong-callee shape, for C name-only resolution."  # e11b
